>
          <p>This article compares two tools, <a href=3D"https://github.com=
/google/sanitizers">Sanitizers</a> and <a href=3D"https://www.valgrind.org/=
">Valgrind</a>, that find memory bugs in programs written in <a href=3D"htt=
ps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Memory_safety">memory-unsafe</a> languages. Thes=
e two tools work in very different ways. Therefore, while Sanitizers (devel=
oped by Google engineers) presents several advantages over Valgrind, each h=
as strengths and weaknesses. Note that the Sanitizers project has a plural =
name because the suite consists of several tools, which we will explore in =
this article.</p>

<p>Memory-checking tools are for memory-unsafe languages such as <a href=3D=
"https://developers.redhat.com/topics/c">C and C++</a>, not for Java, Pytho=
n, and similar memory-safe languages. In memory-unsafe languages, it is eas=
y to mistakenly write past the end of a memory buffer or read memory after =
it has been freed. Programs containing such bugs might run flawlessly most =
of the time and crash only very rarely. Catching these bugs is difficult, w=
hich is why we need tools for that purpose.</p>

<p>Valgrind imposes a much higher slowdown on programs than Sanitizers. A p=
rogram running under Valgrind could run 20 to 50 times slower than in regul=
ar production. This can be a showstopper for CPU-intensive programs. The sl=
owdown for Sanitizers is generally two to four times worse than regular pro=
duction. Instead of Valgrind, you can specify the use of Sanitizers during =
compilation.</p>

<p>This article is divided into the following sections:</p>

<ul>
	<li><a href=3D"https://developers.redhat.com/blog/2021/05/05/memory-error-=
checking-in-c-and-c-comparing-sanitizers-and-valgrind#tldr">A quick Sanitiz=
ers how-to</a></li>
	<li><a href=3D"https://developers.redhat.com/blog/2021/05/05/memory-error-=
