nce, because otherwise it is difficult to find the bug. I therefore use <co=
de>-fno-sanitize-recover=3Dall</code>. I also prefer to extend the Undefine=
dBehaviorSanitizer coverage a bit by including: <code>-fsanitize=3Dfloat-di=
vide-by-zero -fsanitize=3Dfloat-cast-overflow</code>.</p>

<h2>LSAN: Memory leaks</h2>

<p>LeakSanitizer reports allocated memory that has not been freed before th=
e program finished. (See the <a href=3D"https://clang.llvm.org/docs/LeakSan=
itizer.html">LeakSanitizer documentation</a>.) Such behavior <a href=3D"htt=
ps://stackoverflow.com/q/654754/2995591">is not necessarily a bug</a>. But =
freeing all allocated memory makes it easier, for example, to catch real, u=
nexpected memory leaks:</p>

<pre><code>$ cat &gt;leak.cpp &lt;&lt;'EOF'
#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;
int main() {
  void *p =3D malloc(10);
  return p =3D=3D nullptr;
}
EOF
$ clang++ -o leak leak.cpp -Wall -g <strong>-fsanitize=3Daddress</strong>
$ ./leak
