<h2>Sanitizers' interaction with _FORTIFY_SOURCE</h2>

<p>By default, <code>rpmbuild</code> uses the <code>-Wp,-D_FORTIFY_SOURCE=
=3D2</code> option, which implements its own kind of memory access sanity c=
hecking. Unfortunately, it disables some of the memory checks done by Addre=
ssSanitizer. This problem <a href=3D"https://github.com/google/sanitizers/i=
ssues/247">might be fixed in the future</a>. Currently, to prepare for chec=
king by Sanitizers, just disable <code>_FORTIFY_SOURCE</code> using <code>-=
Wp,-U_FORTIFY_SOURCE</code> (which is a more universal form of the simple <=
code>-D_FORTIFY_SOURCE=3D0</code>):</p>

<pre><code>$ cat &gt;strcpyfrom.spec &lt;&lt;'EOF'
Summary: strcpyfrom
Name: strcpyfrom
Version: 1
Release: 1
License: GPLv3+
%description
%build
cat &gt;strcpyfrom.c &lt;&lt;'EOH'
#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;
#include &lt;string.h&gt;
int main(void) {
  char *s =3D malloc(1);
  char d[0x1000];
  strcpy(d, s);
  return 0;
}
EOH
gcc -o strcpyfrom strcpyfrom.c $RPM_OPT_FLAGS <strong>-fsanitize=3Daddress<=
/strong>
echo no error caught:
./strcpyfrom
gcc -o strcpyfrom strcpyfrom.c $RPM_OPT_FLAGS <strong>-fsanitize=3Daddress =
-Wp,-U_FORTIFY_SOURCE</strong>
echo error caught:
./strcpyfrom
EOF
